      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to first determine the dimensions of the original right cone and then use the given information to find the height of the smaller cone.\n\n### Step 1: Determine the radius of the base\n\nSince the circumference of the base is \\(16\\pi\\) inches, we can use the formula for the circumference of a circle:\n\\[\nC = 2\\pi r\n\\]\nwhere \\(C\\) is the circumference and \\(r\\) is the radius. Setting \\(C = 16\\pi\\):\n\\[\n2\\pi r = 16\\pi\n\\]\nSolving for \\(r\\):\n\\[\nr = \\frac{16\\pi}{2\\pi} = 8 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Determine the slant height of the original cone\n\nThe slant height \\(s\\) can be found using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\ns = \\sqrt{r^2 + h^2}\n\\]\nwhere \\(h\\) is the height of the original cone. Given \\(h = 30\\) inches:\n\\[\ns = \\sqrt{8^2 + 30^2} = \\sqrt{64 + 900} = \\sqrt{964} = 2\\sqrt{241} \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Determine the radius of the smaller cone\n\nThe smaller cone has the same base radius as the original cone (8 inches), but its height is reduced. Let \\(h'\\) be the height of the smaller cone. Using the volume formula for a cone:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h'\n\\]\nThe volume of the original cone is:\n\\[\nV_{\\text{original}} = \\frac{1}{3}\\pi (8^2) (30) = \\frac{1}{3}\\pi (64) (30) = 640\\pi \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nThe volume of the smaller cone is given as \\(192\\pi\\) cubic inches. Therefore:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{3}\\pi (8^2) h' = 192\\pi\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{3}\\pi (64) h' = 192\\pi\n\\]\n\\[\n64 h' = 576\n\\]\n\\[\nh' = \\frac{576}{64} = 9 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Determine the ratio of the heights\n\nThe original height was 30 inches, and the new height is 9 inches. The ratio of the shorter height to the original height is:\n\\[\n\\frac{h'}{h} = \\frac{9}{30} = \\frac{3}{10}\n\\]\n\nSo, the ratio is $\\boxed{\\frac{3}{10}}$.",
