      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by identifying the variables and the formulas we will use. We know that the circumference of the base of the original cone is given as \\(16\\pi\\) inches. The formula for the circumference of a circle is:\n\n\\[\nC = 2\\pi r\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(r\\) is the radius of the base. Plugging in the given circumference:\n\n\\[\n16\\pi = 2\\pi r\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this equation by dividing both sides by \\(2\\pi\\):\n\n\\[\nr = 8 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean theorem to find the slant height \\(l\\) of the original cone. The slant height is the hypotenuse of a right triangle formed by the height \\(h\\) (which is 30 inches) and the radius \\(r\\) (which is 8 inches). Therefore:\n\n\\[\nl = \\sqrt{h^2 + r^2} = \\sqrt{30^2 + 8^2} = \\sqrt{900 + 64} = \\sqrt{964} = 2\\sqrt{241} \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nThe volume \\(V\\) of a cone is given by:\n\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the known values into the volume formula:\n\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8^2) c = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (64) c\n\\]\n\nWe know the volume of the shorter cone is \\(192\\pi\\) cubic inches, so we set up the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{3} \\pi (64) c = 192\\pi\n\\]\n\nWe can cancel \\(\\pi\\) and simplify the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{64c}{3} = 192\n\\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by 3 to clear the fraction:\n\n\\[\n64c = 576\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(c\\):\n\n\\[\nc = \\frac{576}{64} = 9\n\\]\n\nThis means the radius of the base of the shorter cone is 9 inches. Now, we use the same slant height \\(l = 2\\sqrt{241}\\) for the shorter cone. Using the Pythagorean theorem again to find the shorter height \\(h'\\):\n\n\\[\nh'^2 + r^2 = (2\\sqrt{241})^2\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(r = 9\\):\n\n\\[\nh'^2 + 9^2 = 482\n\\]\n\n\\[\nh'^2 + 81 = 482\n\\]\n\nSubtracting 81 from both sides:\n\n\\[\nh'^2 = 301\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides:\n\n\\[\nh' = \\sqrt{301} \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nTo find the ratio of the shorter height to the original height, we use the shorter height \\(h' = \\sqrt{301}\\) and the original height \\(h = 30\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{h'}{h} = \\frac{\\sqrt{301}}{30}\n\\]\n\nSo the ratio of the shorter height to the original height is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{301}}{30}}\n\\]",
